        "id": 1561651
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-09-27T02:47:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m having some second thoughts here. What if we changed the logic in `selinux_android_file_context_handle` to transparently do the caching? This would avoid adding a very similar function to the library.\n\nThe main drawback is that we are changing the behaviour of that function. I couldn\u0027t think about a blocking use-case here. Is there a process known to force the reloading of the file_contexts which relies on this behaviour? I don\u0027t think `init` does, nor does file_contexts in apexes, but I might be wrong?",
      "revId": "9f16e0318502adfc15afe68a0216a6b3090fd00f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b4bf559b_79ba68c5",
        "filename": "libselinux/include/selinux/android.h",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 15,
      "author": {
        "id": 1561651
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-09-27T02:47:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We should be able to mark this function as deprecated in favour of your new function. Could you add a comment here and reference the new function as the alternative? You can also include a warning that calling this function twice will reparse the configuration files and hence have a performance impact. Thanks!",
      "revId": "9f16e0318502adfc15afe68a0216a6b3090fd00f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "16bb516b_f7f9fa96",
        "filename": "libselinux/include/selinux/android.h",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 15,
      "author": {
        "id": 1967151
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-01T22:00:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I like this idea. Do you now if there\u0027s a way for me to verify or if there\u0027s a good person to ask?",
      "parentUuid": "89d51108_3bd45b18",
      "revId": "9f16e0318502adfc15afe68a0216a6b3090fd00f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "72bf6e56_5831b83c",
        "filename": "libselinux/include/selinux/android.h",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 15,
      "author": {
        "id": 1561651
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-02T04:14:47Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "@jeffv@google.com @nnk@google.com \n\nWe are discussing the idea of changing the behaviour of `selinux_android_file_context_handle` to automatically cache its result into the internal file sehandle.\n\nThat function is taking a static list of paths for file_contexts (see file_context_paths https://source.corp.google.com/h/googleplex-android/platform/superproject/main/+/main:external/selinux/libselinux/src/android/android_seapp.c;l\u003d25;drc\u003d9666135cc1fd6103a931900b3d64c035c7918b92) and load them. This list is not updated at runtime. I\u0027m assuming that all these partitions (/system, /vendor, /odm, /product, /system_ext) are available before init or any other process call this function (this would be the only reason why the caching would return an incorrect result: one of these partition is mounted in between the first and second call).\n\nOne scenario to consider is when using OverlayFS on `userdebug` images. From my understanding, it allows part of /system to be replaced (especially the SELinux policy). But from what I can tell, this is happening before any of the policy is loaded. So I assume the following series of events is not possible:\n1. init calls selinux_android_file_context_handle\n2. the same init process mount the overlay fs for /system\n3. the same init process calls selinux_android_file_context_handle but gets the cached result.\n\n@yochiang@google.com is my understanding of OverlayFS correct? Can you see any edge case to consider? Thanks.",
      "parentUuid": "16bb516b_f7f9fa96",
      "revId": "9f16e0318502adfc15afe68a0216a6b3090fd00f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d2304004_b9502ba5",
        "filename": "libselinux/include/selinux/android.h",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 15,
      "author": {
        "id": 1054468
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-02T09:41:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "SGTM. Android does not support updating the selinux policy at run time, so I think it\u0027s fine to assume that it won\u0027t happen.",
      "parentUuid": "72bf6e56_5831b83c",
      "revId": "9f16e0318502adfc15afe68a0216a6b3090fd00f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f36ebfb1_6a8c95f0",
        "filename": "libselinux/include/selinux/android.h",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 15,
      "author": {
        "id": 1337669
      },
      "writtenOn": "2024-10-08T07:58:57Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Both the filesystem and overlayFS of /system (and /vendor, /odm ... etc) should be mounted during init-first-stage. So I believe the scenario you described indeed should not happen. What should happen is:\n\n1. init (first stage) mount /system and overlayFS of /system\n2. init (first stage) execv init (second stage)\n3. init (second stage) calls selinux_android_file_context_handle",
      "parentUuid": "d2304004_b9502ba5",
      "revId": "9f16e0318502adfc15afe68a0216a6b3090fd00f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
